  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Late-inning rallies are keeping this team afloat, and it's becoming unsustainable.",
    "what_this_means": "Soderstrom tied it in the ninth with a single, then won it in the tenth. That sequence matters because the Athletics didn't control this game. They chased it from the second inning onward, trailed for six straight innings, and found themselves down to their final strike. Aaron Civale lasted five innings and left with the team behind. The bullpen. Medina, Leiter Jr., Barlow, and Harris. Threw five scoreless to give the bats a window. When that window opened, Soderstrom closed it. He went 2-for-5 with three RBI and delivered the moment the team needed. This is a team learning to win games it doesn't deserve to win. They've now done it twice in two days. That math gets harder every time.",
    "what_to_watch": "Jos\u00e9 Soriano takes the ball tonight for the Angels. His 2.41 ERA has been the core of LAA's pitching identity, and he's facing a lineup hitting .251 overall. A matchup that favors the visitor's arm, not the visitor's depth.",
